Calc: the .xlsx export keeps number cells as packed runs of their row (16 bytes per cell instead of a record).

The export created one XclExpRkCell or XclExpNumberCell record per number cell and held them all until the sheet
was written. An RK record only merges with an RK cell right next to it, so in rows of mixed cells nearly every
number had its own record: 1.5 million number cells took about 150 MB at the peak of saving a 50 MB workbook of
formulas, about 600 MB for 200 MB. For .xlsx a finished number cell needs only its value and its cell format
identifier: that is all XclExpNumberCell::SaveXml and XclExpRkCell::WriteXmlContents write.

Each number cell is still created as before, so the cell formats are registered in the same order; the finished
record is then replaced by a run entry holding the value the record writes (for an RK cell the value decoded from
its RK form), and adjacent entries of a row merge through the existing TryMerge hook. Not used for the binary
formats, for Boolean cells or for the base cell of a merged range.
New logic in sc/source/filter/inc/hstxlsxpackednumbers.hxx; hooks: 11 lines in xetable.cxx.
The environment variable HST_SC_XLSX_PACKED_NUMBERS_OFF keeps one record per number cell (used by the test to
compare both paths).

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new file is LicenseRef-HST-Proprietary.
Test: CppunitTest_sc_subsequent_export_test6 testXlsxPackedNumberCellsWriteTheSameSheet saves one document on
both paths (RK integers and hundredths, doubles, large values, runs interrupted by text, a formula and a blank
cell, bold, percent and date formats, a Boolean, a merged base, a hidden row, a long row) and compares sheet1.xml,
sharedStrings.xml, styles.xml and workbook.xml; the corpus and the large fixtures are saved on both paths.
